size the layout from the panel, at runtime, so one binary fits both

This port makes `OUT_W`/`OUT_H` the panel rather than upstream's 720x480
canvas, which is what lets the shelf and the menus use the whole screen. They
were `const`, so the panel was a build: a Mini Flip binary at 752x560 and a
Mini Plus one at 640x480, and a card that only ran on the handheld it was
built for.

They are two atomics now, defaulting to the Flip's panel and set once by
`device_app` from the framebuffer before any of the UI is built. Reads are
Relaxed: the value is written before the render and input threads exist and
nothing writes it again.

The cascade is the cost. Fifteen constants folded a panel dimension in and had
to become functions -- `Canvas::FRAMED` the widest of them at 137 call sites,
plus `SLOT_W`, `PORT_W`, `FOOTER_Y`, `PORT_Y`, `CX`, `ARCS`, `CLICKS_X/Y`,
`ARROW_LEFT_X`/`ARROW_RIGHT_X`, `BAND_Y`, `BAY_Y`, `LIP_Y`, `SLIT_Y`,
`SCOOP_Y`/`SCOOP_D`, `SEATED_Y`, `SEATED_TIP`, `CORE_LEGEND_Y` and
`LINK_LEGEND_Y`. 58 files, nearly all of it mechanical.

Three device-specific sizes come with it, because a narrower panel than the
Flip's is what this is for:

  - **The picture is fitted to the panel's width.** GBA fills its texture, so
    3x is 720 wide and on a 640x480 panel that cropped 40px off each side --
    the lap counter and the clock in Mario Kart, read off the device. It
    scales to 2.667x instead. A no-op at 752, where 720 already fits.

  - **The console's body is sized from the picture's frame, not the panel.**
    Upstream's `panel_bezel` takes the frame from `screen_rect`, which reads
    it out of `OUT_W`/`OUT_H` -- true for upstream, where those *are* the
    game, and false here, where they are the panel. That made it cover the
    panel and crop the art: the Game Boy's battery lamp went off the left
    edge of a Mini Plus. `frame_in` takes the frame from the picture instead
    and `fill_panel` extends the plastic outwards to fill the rest, which is
    the approach upstream arrived at independently in `panel_bezel` and the
    one this patch used before the f4b7bc3 rebase.

  - **A picture shorter than the panel sits at the top when the Advance SP
    shell is behind it.** The shell's opening is rows 6..421 of its 640x480
    art with a 58px chin of lettering below, measured off the file; centring
    a 427-tall picture put its last rows under the chin. Upstream says the
    same in `bezel::game_rect`, which puts y at 0 when the frame is shown.

`fill_panel` tracks the art's real extent rather than assuming the margins
match. They do not when the gap is odd -- 427 into 480 leaves 26 above and 27
below -- and reading one row past what the art wrote put an unwritten black
line through the plastic at y=453, mirrored again at 475. Two tests pin it,
one per axis.

Tested on both handhelds from the same card: a Mini Flip at 752x560 and a
Mini Plus at 640x480, shelf and game, with the console bodies and the SP
shell drawn right on each.

Applies to upstream 91499a9.

